    <p class="plate-note">Three slices, and only the gold one is made of anything anyone has ever held.
    The percentages are energy density today; they are not the percentages at any other moment, which is
    the whole hinge of this page. <span class="sci">&#9670;</span></p>
  </figure>

  <div class="grid two">
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>How each one is weighed</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Ordinary matter</strong> from the relative heights of
        the odd and even peaks in the CMB power spectrum, and independently from the deuterium abundance
        left over from the first three minutes. Two completely different methods, same answer.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Dark matter</strong> from the position and height of
        the third peak, from galaxy rotation curves, from cluster lensing, and from the Bullet Cluster,
        where the mass and the gas are visibly in different places.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Dark energy</strong> from the dimming of distant type
        Ia supernovae, from the flat geometry the CMB demands, and from the baryon acoustic scale.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="frame card">
      <h3>What is honestly unresolved</h3>
      <ul class="biblio">
        <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Nobody knows what dark matter is.</strong> Every
        direct-detection experiment so far has come back empty.</li>
        <li><span class="sci">&#9670;</span> <strong>Nobody knows what dark energy is.</strong> The
        cosmological constant fits, and the vacuum-energy calculation that should produce it overshoots by
        up to 120 orders of magnitude.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> <strong>The Hubble tension.</strong> Early-universe and
        late-universe measurements of the expansion rate disagree at around five sigma, and nobody has
        closed it.</li>
        <li><span class="spec">&#9670;</span> <strong>DESI 2024&ndash;25</strong> reports a mild preference
        for dark energy that weakens with time. Mild. Watch it; do not spend it.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

